No README change at all. Let's look at the full files to understand raw_id derivation and feeds/fixtures.
Let's set up a scratch copy and actually run it to confirm the crash and dedup behavior.
Confirmed: blogroll items (no stable `raw_id`) are resent on every run forever — this is the exact bug the task asked to fix, still present for one of the three feeds. Let's check snapshot-b for the guid-rotation case (generic/"wire" feed with edited items) and confirm newsroom/wire dedup works correctly.
Confirmed: no blogroll rows ever appear in `sent_items` (NOT NULL constraint silently swallowed by `INSERT OR IGNORE`), and the wire item's rotated guid (`...-0031-r2`) is recorded as a brand-new send. Now writing the review.
Review written to `_review.md`. Summary of findings:

The agent added a `sent_items(raw_id, channel)` table with per-channel dedup, which is the right shape of solution and a minimal, respectful diff — but it never handles the `blogroll` feed's `raw_id = None` case (explicitly commented on in `feeds.py`), so those items are **never** recorded as sent and get re-delivered on every single run, forever. I verified this by running the code twice against the same fixture snapshot: the same blogroll items reappeared in the "Ops digest" output both times, with zero blogroll rows ever landing in `sent_items`. The `generic`/wire feed's guid-rotation-on-edit problem (also documented in a pre-existing comment) is likewise unaddressed — an edited item's rotated guid is treated as brand new. README is byte-identical to the original; the only documentation is a docstring claiming "cron re-runs never send duplicates," which is demonstrably false for one of three feeds.

Score: **21.5/40**, `would_merge: no` — the core complaint is only partially fixed and the failure is silent and undocumented.
